\\pi }{2}].\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302102 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b5e907a2-031b-5216-92f5-5f4ff0100ab5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300865}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b5e907a2-031b-5216-92f5-5f4ff0100ab5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300865} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ec9b74a-062d-5eb7-8cce-bb0a8247b758" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301681} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39126ced-d9b7-5da2-ad4a-fae678eab433" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300238}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "39126ced-d9b7-5da2-ad4a-fae678eab433" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300238} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf0ca3ae-534e-533c-86b9-d3973cf4037c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301891}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d06f28ce-407e-57f3-9468-51dcff74f6bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-321976" :question/problem "![](images/fb/fbe6faf7f14f9ce51c299f18f9596c08c650079d2ca23398180baff8ea965553.png)\n\nThe three-quarter-circle above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$ Assume $\\theta $ is between $0$ and $2\\pi .$\n\n$\\theta \\in [${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$]$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +a,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +b,\\,\\theta \\in (0,2\\pi ].\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have a circle of radius $1$ centered at the point $(-1,1).$ So, the parametric equations of the *full* circle are \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1,\\,\\theta \\in (0,2\\pi ].\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a three-quarter-circle, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the circle and the line parallel to the $x$-axis and crossing the center of the circle. \n\n![](images/28/28d5eaee4de3a6100a92fa017b71d9860ea19cd7073284b41511dda4155ecb6d.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[\\frac{\\pi }{2},2\\pi ]$, we get the required three-quarter-circle.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the three-quarter circle are \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1,\\,\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},2\\pi ].\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348165 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10bc0c2e-b811-53c7-9a39-a3a742a76a4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{4}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345494} {:answer/id #uuid "16bdfa20-b41b-5459-b61c-04f948ff3534" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345552} {:answer/id #uuid "58da2168-0c36-5490-ba1e-943612f0bccd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{5\\pi }{4}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346116} {:answer/id #uuid "918f45dd-e507-51a9-9661-5c366ab69a9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346623} {:answer/id #uuid "d06d8135-2704-5d9a-a92a-9f45781af4eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347178}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "918f45dd-e507-51a9-9661-5c366ab69a9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\t