nswer/id #uuid "14ac3088-b004-5942-a759-305e66bd829a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275671} {:answer/id #uuid "6de4ee6e-8cf1-5e32-9477-62eec85dc8e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276459} {:answer/id #uuid "84c9d846-ee11-5df6-b4f3-ad301f646c61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276667} {:answer/id #uuid "a2132d3d-04bc-511e-8fa8-498a58febd95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+1)^{2}+(y+2)^{2}=\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276936} {:answer/id #uuid "e7431f1c-0e17-521f-8eb7-8db114d02b28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}+(y+1)^{2}=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277497}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "14ac3088-b004-5942-a759-305e66bd829a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275671} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb6ca943-4b04-5351-9712-89b82d0fb09b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278169}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "aceee4b9-7657-5a9b-99d8-00f2a88fd80d" :question/math-academy-id "q-15845" :question/problem "Determine the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1,\\,y=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-1 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 2(x-1) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 4(x-1)^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-2 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 2(y-2) & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 4(y-2)^{2} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(x-1)^{2}+4(y-2)^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 4(x-1)^{2}+4(y-2)^{2} & =1 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence the Cartesian equation of the circle is $(x-1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=\\frac{1}{4}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292837 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a911054-c5bb-5029-8afa-8d953ec079d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290743}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a911054-c5bb-5029-8afa-8d953ec079d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290743} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ff116d0-793f-5dec-9b7a-1f999289cb92" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291853} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4fa8b2ec-7940-569c-bacf-94019cc63207" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290972}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4fa8b2ec-7940-569c-bacf-94019cc63207" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290972} :answer-field/id #uuid "485d6ab9-cde1-5618-b061-0978e3c079c7" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291967} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fbc666df-928f-50c4-b815-3ae4f2924c93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291702}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fbc666df-928f-50c4-b815-3ae4f2924c93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291702} :answer-field/id #uuid "b20085d3-a7a3-553b-a6da-d04d5f933768" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292284}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "52360184-f3de-54ee-a0c9-a89eddeb575c" :question/math-academy-id "q-275313" :question/problem "The Cartesian equation of the circle defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi \n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$(x\\,-$\n{{field-1}}$)^{2}+(y\\,+$\n{{field-2}}$)^{2}=$\n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-2}{6} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{{6}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{36} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y+1}{6} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{{6}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{36} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{36}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{36} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{36}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{36} & =1 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+(y+1)^{2} & =36\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation is $(x-2)^{2}+(y+1)^{2}=36.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302103 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b4ea447-f2d0-526c-9ddf-83da34800f28" :an