=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{25}{(x+3)}^{2}+\\frac{4}{25}{(y-1)}^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{4}{25}{(x+3)}^{2}+\\frac{4}{25}{(y-1)}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {(x+3)}^{2}+{(y-1)}^{2} & =\\frac{25}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation is ${(x+3)}^{2}+{(y-1)}^{2}=\\frac{25}{4}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302105 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e4625cd-b5b3-5b8d-9670-dd9b56b8ac39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300369}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e4625cd-b5b3-5b8d-9670-dd9b56b8ac39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300369} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f3a6527-bf58-56ec-a72b-37753e4a00d9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301485} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3efba986-59fe-5477-a7a1-a5717686607f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300271}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3efba986-59fe-5477-a7a1-a5717686607f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300271} :answer-field/id #uuid "d63d177c-ca19-544d-97d0-9ab8f401a7fe" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301913} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8dc15257-2218-54e8-81b4-38b448e8f019" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300679}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8dc15257-2218-54e8-81b4-38b448e8f019" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300679} :answer-field/id #uuid "df06b6c6-e63e-5bfa-a5f2-b0b512067676" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301935}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "553d8835-419a-5f16-a1a2-9829041916d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-321989" :question/problem "The Cartesian equation of the circle defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\frac{7}{2},\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\frac{11}{4},\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi \n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$(x\\,-$\n{{field-1}}$)^{2}+(y\\,+$\n{{field-2}}$)^{2}=$\n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-\\frac{7}{2} & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}(x-\\frac{7}{2}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{1}{9}{(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+\\frac{11}{4} & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}(y+\\frac{11}{4}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{1}{9}{(y+\\frac{11}{4})}^{2} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{9}{(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{9}{(y+\\frac{11}{4})}^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{1}{9}{(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{9}{(y+\\frac{11}{4})}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+{(y+\\frac{11}{4})}^{2} & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation is ${(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+{(y+\\frac{11}{4})}^{2}=9.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302106 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ac5b1ab-ce4b-58ec-b400-f54e30c65791" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300740}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ac5b1ab-ce4b-58ec-b400-f54e30c65791" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300740} :answer-field/id #uuid "42c7c8d4-2568-5cb9-818c-fa1a1b025b85" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301452} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6032e270-5212-5635-b7b2-6e7482fd04fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300455}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6032e270-5212-5635-b7b2-6e7482fd04fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300455} :answer-field/id #uuid "60aa9e9f-825a-5d6f-bd0b-da172d0a0aa7" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301536} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f4430592-e773-501c-a3c7-b83467548263" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "49" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301169}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f4430592-e773-501c-a3c7-b83467548263" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "49" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301169} :answer-field/id #uuid "fed2c7bd-b2ec-5d9b-8a43-de0831700108" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302038}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "dd0e867b-d505-5af8-8b82-05070f856f71" :question/math-academy-id "q-321991" :question/problem "The Cartesian equation of the circle defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -5,\\,y=7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +13,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi \n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$(x\\,+$\n{{field-1}}$)^{2}+(y\\,-$\n{{field-2}}$)^{2}=$\n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+5 & =7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+5)^{2}}{{7}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{(x+5)}^{2}}{49} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-13 & =7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y-13)^{2}}{{7}^{2}} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{(y-13)}^{2}}{49} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{(x+5)}^{2}}{49}+\\frac{{(y-13)}^{2}}{49} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{(x+5)}^{2}}{49}+\\frac{{(y-13)}^{2}}{49} & =1 \\\\ {(x+5)}^{2}+{(y-13)}^{2} & =49.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation is ${(x+5)}^{2}+{(y-13)}^{2}=49.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348178 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bb599ef-8a81-52a5-8b28-084af574bb02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345442} {:answer/id #uuid "39105a8b-6d43-5d27-870f-eea96d04d066" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345837} {:answer/id #uuid "b51e4691-b8a5-59dd-812a-632868d6034a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=56" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346929} {:answer/id #uuid "b869bfc2-922d-50cc-8561-1dc9c77a11db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=49" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346955} {:answer/id #uuid "f2fac58b-c9cb-5f3e-8265-5ba360717b7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=\\sqrt{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347500}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b869bfc2-922d-50cc-8561-1dc9c77a11db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=49" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346955} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d2a1c5c-432e-561b-bbf6-4b53c4971d96" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347678}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e9c6c4b8-31e2-55b5-bdea-fe6d4fa5f314" :question/math-academy-id "q-79219" :question/problem "Calculate the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$$\nx=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy squaring the $x$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {x}^{2} & =(7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2} \\\\  & =49{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy squaring the $y$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {y}^{2} & =(7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} \\\\  & =49{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =49{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +49{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =49({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =49(1) \\\\  & =49.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=49.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348179 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29742ccd-2a8b-5830-965f-56c1337dc0c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-1)^{2}+(y+4)^{2}=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345720} {:answer/id #uuid "6bffa286-3722-571f-9dfc-45f46dd9445a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+1)^{2}+(y-4)^{2}=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346287} {:answer/id #uuid "a3c605c0-4210-5da8-bf0e-68e9bc14a673" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-1)^{2}+(y+4)^{2}=8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346773} {:answer/id #uuid "e8b7483d-d07d-5c30-911e-fead4c73b24d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+1)^{2}+(y-4)^{2}=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347402} {:answer/id #uuid "e8c9e650-e286-56f7-989b-7e9f695fdfdd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-1)^{2}+(y+4)^{2}=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347404}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6bffa286-3722-571f-9dfc-45f46dd9445a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+1)^{2}+(y-4)^{2}=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346287} :answer-field/id #uuid "9017fea1-b2a3-55d2-b708-0851cf5e9c44" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347854}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7d81b068-8ac5-5409-b15b-c98e1cd9146e" :question/math-academy-id "q-79220" :question/problem "Determine the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +4,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+1}{2} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{4} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y-4}{2} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{4} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{4} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ (x+1)^{2}+(y-4)^{2} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is $(x+1)^{2}+(y-4)^{2}=4.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348180 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b2f63f3-85a4-55bf-a395-74537aff84ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+2)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345587} {:answer/id #uuid "35590547-774e-5b38-9b3b-c11f13078f10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+2)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345812} {:answer/id #uuid "97a231cb-9741-5f6d-bdb9-9a1deed0940a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}+(y-3)^{2}=9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346679} {:answer/id #uuid "c93fa12b-ab82-59d4-918f-7a96fbadc4c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+2)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347104} {:answer/id #uuid "cccf5f9e-fccf-5706-aeb7-33fe7e70deb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}+(y-3)^{2}=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347143}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "35590547-774e-5b38-9b3b-c11f13078f10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+2)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345812} :answer-field/id #uuid "23c2e10e-3b72-5e83-a5e0-5d16c4723896" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347665}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "97d013bd-90fe-54ca-b491-25e886bc0e3f" :question/math-academy-id "q-79221" :question/problem "Determine the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+2}{3} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{9} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y+3}{3} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{9} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{9} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ (x+2)^{2}+(y+3)^{2} & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is $(x+2)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=9.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348181 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1042167e-00ed-5fbc-88d2-94fc33f5b270" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-5)^{2}+{y}^{2}=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345489} {:answer/id #uuid "1dd3385e-97f4-5beb-9921-7351f3e80016" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+5)^{2}+{y}^{2}=16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345609} {:answer/id #uuid "306c7503-2c5c-583e-98ed-907fcfc9c71e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-5)^{2}+(y-4)^{2}=16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345782} {:answer/id #uuid "7dda351a-9d22-5b67-a478-1ea7b7183df7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-5)^{2}+{y}^{2}=16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346451} {:answer/id #uuid "90339e5b-c896-5b0e-a352-e21f8b2f83c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+5)^{2}+(y+4)^{2}=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346608}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7dda351a-9d22-5b67-a478-1ea7b7183df7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-5)^{2}+{y}^{2}=16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346451} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2d884e0-029c-51cf-8515-61cb5d0cef63" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347975}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "880981df-79aa-5ac9-b5ac-a6e8ffed14a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-79222" :question/problem "Determine the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +5,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-5}{4} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x-5)^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x-5)^{2}}{16} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{4} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-5)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x-5)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ (x-5)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is $(x-5)^{2}+{y}^{2}=16.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Parametrized Circle"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087819 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087654 :question/id #uuid "5c3b6d53-3dbb-59f6-8d15-a9e6f48927a1" :question/math-academy-id "e-9942" :question/problem "![](images/9a/9af4801abdec165de2b720a21dd6a727c578e32cbae9c9c54bd589a1b65043b0.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows train routes between three train stations, where the nodes represent stations and arrows represent the directed routes between them. The (incomplete) route matrix below represents the number of routes between the three stations. Find the value of $a+b+c.$\n\n|  |  | To: |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **A** | **B** | **C** |\n| From: | **A** | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}a}$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n| **B** | $0$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}b}$ | $2$ |  |\n| **C** | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}c}$ | $0$ | $1$ |  |" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our diagram.\n\n- There is $1$ arrow starting at Station A and ending at Station A. So, there is only one route from $A$ to $A,$ and so $a=1.$\n\n- No arrows are starting at Station B and ending at Station B. So, there are no routes from $B$ to $B,$ and so $b=0.$\n\n- There is $1$ arrow starting at Station C and ending at Station A. So, there is only one route from $C$ to $A,$ and so $c=1.$\n\nAs a result, our route matrix is the following:\n\n|  |  | To: |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **A** | **B** | **C** |\n| From: | **A** | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}1}$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n| **B** | $0$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0}$ | $2$ |  |\n| **C** | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}1}$ | $0$ | $1$ |  |\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\na+b+c = 1+0+1 = 2.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "10ca3efc-7d23-5bc1-ab41-f785bed69986" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088004 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c96a891-effe-5393-9122-2fd45c7880a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084759} {:answer/id #uuid "a56af5ae-6c68-5eec-9691-9f0c10aa2a51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084760} {:answer/id #uuid "d3dbeccf-f8e6-5f67-94e6-b71395980682" :ans